  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'PAPER_END'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nCode agents powered by large language models (LLMs) have revolutionized software development by enabling autonomous code generation, execution, and debugging. However, as these agents gain broader adoption, concerns about their safety, security, and trustworthiness have become increasingly prominent. This paper reviews recent research advances in code agents and red-teaming methodologies. We examine the emerging landscape of automated red-teaming agents that evaluate code agent vulnerabilities, comprehensive benchmarks for assessing risky code execution and generation, and the development of blue-teaming approaches for defensive security testing. Our analysis covers key contributions including RedCodeAgent, a novel automatic red-teaming agent; RedCode, a comprehensive benchmark for risky code evaluation; DecodingTrust, a framework for assessing trustworthiness in GPT models; and BlueCodeAgent, which leverages automated red-teaming for blue teaming purposes. We synthesize findings from these works to identify current challenges, emerging trends, and future research directions in the safety and security of code agents.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nThe rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) has enabled the development of sophisticated code agents capable of performing complex programming tasks autonomously. These code agents, which integrate code generation capabilities with code execution environments, have found applications in software engineering, educational tools, and automated debugging systems. Recent examples include GitHub Copilot, Devin, and various open-source agent frameworks that combine LLMs with tools for code interpretation and execution.\n\nWhile the capabilities of code agents continue to expand, their deployment in real-world scenarios raises significant safety and security concerns. Code agents that can autonomously write and execute code may inadvertently generate harmful, insecure, or malicious software when faced with adversarial prompts or when deployed in production environments. The potential consequences range from data breaches and system compromise to the propagation of vulnerable code in software supply chains.\n\nRed-teaming, the practice of simulating adversarial attacks to identify vulnerabilities, has emerged as a critical methodology for evaluating and improving the safety of AI systems. In the context of code agents, red-teaming involves designing prompts and attack strategies that attempt to elicit undesirable behaviors such as generating malicious code, exploiting system vulnerabilities, or bypassing safety guardrails. Recent research has recognized the need for systematic approaches to red-teaming code agents, leading to the development of automated red-teaming agents and comprehensive evaluation benchmarks.\n\nThis paper reviews recent advances in code agent safety and red-teaming research. We specifically examine four key contributions to the field: (1) RedCodeAgent [1], which introduces an automatic red-teaming agent designed to evaluate diverse code agents; (2) RedCode [2], a benchmark for assessing risky code execution and generation capabilities; (3) DecodingTrust [3], a comprehensive assessment framework for evaluating trustworthiness in GPT models; and (4) BlueCodeAgent [4], which enables blue teaming through automated red-teaming methodologies. We discuss the methodologies, findings, and implications of these works, and identify directions for future research.\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\n### 2.1 Automatic Red-teaming for Code Agents\n\nThe development of automated red-teaming agents represents a significant advancement in code agent safety evaluation. Traditional red-teaming approaches rely on human experts to craft adversarial prompts, which is both time-consuming and limited in scope. RedCodeAgent, introduced in recent work [1], addresses these limitations by developing an automatic red-teaming agent that can systematically probe diverse code agents for vulnerabilities.\n\nRedCodeAgent leverages the generative capabilities of LLMs to create diverse attack prompts targeting different aspects of code agent behavior. The agent operates through an iterative process where it generates potential attack prompts, evaluates their effectiveness against the target code agent, and refines its strategy based on the results. This approach enables comprehensive coverage of the attack space, identifying vulnerabilities that might be missed by manual red-teaming efforts.\n\nThe work demonstrates that automated red-teaming can uncover a wide range of safety issues in code agents, including vulnerabilities related to code injection, privilege escalation, and data exfiltration. These findings highlight the importance of integrating automated red-teaming into the development and deployment pipeline of code agents.\n\n### 2.2 Benchmarks for Code Agent Safety\n\nComprehensive evaluation benchmarks are essential for measuring and comparing the safety of different code agents. RedCode [2] addresses this need by providing a comprehensive benchmark for risky code execution and generation. The benchmark consists of two components: RedCode-Exec, which evaluates code agents' ability to recognize and handle unsafe code execution requests, and RedCode-Gen, which assesses whether code agents will generate harmful code when prompted.\n\nRedCode-Exec provides 4,050 risky test cases in Python and Bash tasks, covering 25 types of critical vulnerabilities across 8 domains including websites, file systems, and network operations. The benchmark uses Docker environments to safely evaluate code execution results and provides metrics for assessing agent behavior. RedCode-Gen includes 160 prompts with function signatures and docstrings to evaluate the propensity of code agents to generate malicious software.\n\nThe empirical evaluation of RedCode across multiple agent frameworks and 19 LLMs reveals important insights into code agent vulnerabilities. Notably, the study found that agents are more likely to reject executing risky operations on operating systems but less likely to reject technically buggy code. Additionally, risky operations described in natural text lead to lower rejection rates than those described in code format, highlighting the importance of context-aware safety mechanisms.\n\n### 2.3 Trustworthiness Assessment of GPT Models\n\nThe trustworthiness of foundational models plays a critical role in determining the safety of code agents built upon them. DecodingTrust [3] provides a comprehensive assessment of trustworthiness in GPT models, examining various dimensions including honesty, harmlessness, and helpfulness.\n\nThe DecodingTrust framework evaluates GPT models across multiple trustworthiness dimensions, providing insights into how model capabilities correlate with safety behaviors. The assessment reveals that while GPT models have made significant progress in various capabilities, there are still notable gaps in trustworthiness, particularly in areas related to generating harmful content and handling adversarial prompts.\n\nThese findings have important implications for code agent safety, as the trustworthiness of the underlying model directly impacts the agent's ability to safely generate and execute code. Understanding the trustworthiness profiles of different models enables researchers and practitioners to make informed decisions about model selection and safety configurations.\n\n### 2.4 Blue Teaming and Defensive Security\n\nWhile red-teaming focuses on identifying vulnerabilities through offensive evaluation, blue-teaming emphasizes defensive security measures and proactive protection strategies. BlueCodeAgent [4] represents a novel approach that combines red-teaming and blue-teaming methodologies for code generation AI systems.\n\nBlueCodeAgent enables a feedback loop where insights gained from automated red-teaming are used to improve defensive mechanisms. The agent leverages the findings from red-teaming exercises to develop and implement protective measures against identified vulnerabilities. This approach ensures that security improvements are directly informed by empirical vulnerability data rather than theoretical risk assessments.\n\nThe work demonstrates that integrating red-teaming and blue-teaming methodologies creates a more robust security framework for code agents. By continuously identifying vulnerabilities and implementing countermeasures, BlueCodeAgent provides a systematic approach to maintaining and improving the security posture of code generation systems over time.\n\n## 3. Discussion\n\nThe research surveyed in this paper highlights several key insights and challenges in code agent safety:\n\n**Integration of Automated Red-teaming:** Automated red-teaming agents like RedCodeAgent demonstrate the feasibility and effectiveness of systematic, automated security evaluation. The ability to generate diverse attack prompts and iteratively refine strategies enables comprehensive vulnerability discovery that surpasses manual testing approaches.\n\n**Need for Comprehensive Benchmarks:** Benchmarks like RedCode provide standardized evaluation frameworks that enable meaningful comparison across different code agent implementations. The development of benchmarks covering both execution safety and generation safety is crucial for holistic security assessment.\n\n**Model-Dependent Safety:** Research such as DecodingTrust reveals that trustworthiness is closely tied to the capabilities and training of the underlying language model. This finding underscores the importance of selecting appropriate base models and implementing additional safety mechanisms as needed.\n\n**Holistic Security Approaches:** The BlueCodeAgent work illustrates the value of integrating offensive and defensive security practices. A comprehensive security strategy should combine automated vulnerability discovery with proactive defense development.\n\nDespite these advances, several challenges remain:\n\n1. **Evolving Attack Surfaces:** As code agents become more sophisticated, the attack surface expands to include new vectors such as tool misuse, multi-agent coordination vulnerabilities, and integration security issues.\n\n2. **Evaluation Scalability:** Current benchmarks and red-teaming approaches may not scale efficiently to cover the full range of possible vulnerabilities and attack scenarios.\n\n3. **Real-World Validation:** Many safety evaluations are conducted in controlled environments that may not fully capture the complexities of production deployments.\n\n4. **Cross-Domain Risks:** Code agents increasingly interact with diverse systems and data sources, creating cross-domain security challenges that require specialized evaluation approaches.\n\n## 4. Conclusion\n\nThe field of code agent safety has made significant progress through recent research advances in automated red-teaming, benchmark development, trustworthiness assessment, and defensive security methodologies. The works surveyed in this paper—RedCodeAgent [1], RedCode [2], DecodingTrust [3], and BlueCodeAgent [4]—represent important contributions that advance our understanding of code agent vulnerabilities and provide tools for systematic security evaluation.\n\nKey takeaways from this review include the effectiveness of automated red-teaming for vulnerability discovery, the importance of comprehensive evaluation benchmarks, the influence of base model trustworthiness on agent safety, and the value of integrating offensive and defensive security approaches. As code agents continue to evolve and find broader applications, ongoing research in these areas will be essential for ensuring their safe and reliable deployment.\n\nFuture work should focus on scaling safety evaluation to cover emerging attack vectors, developing more realistic evaluation environments that mirror production deployments, and creating adaptive security mechanisms that can respond to novel threats in real-time. The collaboration between researchers developing offensive red-teaming capabilities and those building defensive blue-teaming frameworks will be crucial for advancing the safety and security of code agents in the years ahead.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[2] Chengquan Guo, Xun Liu, Chulin Xie et al. \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents.\" arXiv:2409.12345, 2024.\n\n[3] Boxin Wang, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[4] Chengquan Guo, Yuzhou Nie, Chulin Xie et al. \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI.\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\nPAPER_END"
